1.3 GHDL — wired, as a separate program

  **Corrected 2026-09-13.** This section previously read "planned, not yet
  wired" and analysed `ghdl-yosys-plugin`. Both were wrong about the shipped
  architecture.

  VHDL elaboration IS wired. The adapter is not in this repository's `lib/`
  — it lives in the shared `crux_yosys` package (`crux-shared`), which is
  why a search of `lib/` found nothing.

  **`ghdl-yosys-plugin` is not used and is not loaded.** VHDL is lowered to
  Verilog by a standalone `ghdl --synth --out=verilog … -e <top>` run, and
  Yosys then reads the emitted Verilog with `read_verilog`. No `plugin -i
  ghdl` is ever emitted; `crux_yosys`'s runner test asserts the generated
  script contains neither `plugin -i ghdl` nor `read_vhdl`.

  This materially simplifies the licensing picture. The previous analysis
  turned on combining a GPL-3.0 plugin, GPL-2.0 GHDL and ISC Yosys into one
  loaded process. That combination does not occur. What occurs is two
  independent programs, each invoked across a process boundary, exchanging
  files and diagnostic text — the §1.0 analysis, unchanged.

  Component: GHDL
  Role:      VHDL → Verilog lowering, `ghdl --synth --out=verilog`
  License:   GPL-2.0 (GNU General Public License v2.0; the project's
             source headers state "or later")
  Upstream:  https://github.com/ghdl/ghdl
  Copyright: Tristan Gingold and the GHDL contributors
  Pinned:    4.1.0 in `tool/bundled_engines.yaml`

  `tool/bundled_engines.yaml` pins no `ghdl-yosys-plugin`, and the
  bundled-engine fetch workflow neither downloads nor caches one.

